+# Keep sessions in process memory
+
+For whoever changes login, deploys or the session store. Sessions live in the web process's memory because the
+volunteers requested faster requests without a Postgres session query.
+
+## Context
+
+In 2023 larder moved from one server to two behind a load balancer, and sessions stayed in each server's memory.
+Members were logged out whenever a request reached the other server, and every deploy logged out everyone; the pantry
+volunteers stopped deploying during opening hours. Postgres replaced process memory so sessions survived deploys
+and were shared between servers, without another service to run. Each request then read its session from Postgres.
+The volunteers have now requested process-local storage to make the app faster; no measured latency is available
+here. This change favors removing session queries and keeping the service count down over session continuity.
+
+## Options
+
+- **Sticky sessions at the load balancer**: previously rejected because a deploy still logged out everyone on the
+  restarted server; now a possible mitigation for requests reaching different processes, not a persistent store.
+- **Redis as a session store**: fast and shared; rejected because the volunteers would run another service only
+  for sessions.
+- **Postgres**: chosen previously for shared, persistent sessions; replaced to remove session database queries.
+- **A dict in each web process**: chosen to remove session queries without another service.
+
+## Decision
+
+Sessions live in a dict in each web process, exposed as the shared `sessions` instance in
+[`larder/session_store.py`](../../larder/session_store.py). This removes session reads and writes to Postgres
+without introducing another service.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Session access requires no database round trip. The [session limits](../features/sessions.md#known-limits) describe
+the effects on members when requests switch processes or a process restarts. Removing a query does not establish
+an end-to-end speed improvement without measurement.
